A||||17||Shaft||Another Reason not to Street Race||||13||
Z||000000||Shaft||03-22-2001||10:53 PM|||| <A HREF="http://fl.mlive.com/news/index.ssf?/news/stories/20010322f22a2fatalcrash.frm" TARGET=_blank>http://fl.mlive.com/news/index.ssf?/news/stories/20010322f22a2fatalcrash.frm</A> <P>This just happened on Wed. night. Three students from my school were in the car.  The kid that was killed lived down the hall from me. It's sad to see something like this happen, especially to people you pass in the hall everyday. There is no evidence that he was street racing, but he was driving at a very high rate of speed, probably trying to impress his friends. (He drove a newer Trans Am WS-6) I know we all drive fast, especially me, but next time think of this when you do. Especially if you have other people in the car.||||reg||13
Z||000001||Tigger||03-23-2001||02:17 AM||||I seen the story on ABC12 news this morning.  Pretty sad, 18 yrs old, going to Kettering.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||03-23-2001||06:49 PM||||A 12 yr old girl drown in a bowl of Kellogs Cornflakes last weekend.  Does that mean I should stay away from Cornflakes?  What exactly does your post have to do with streetracing? There's idiot drivers everywhere, and the worst of them probably never street race(soccer moms, etc.)  Regardless of how safe you are, there's always threat of an accident.  All you can do is use common sense... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shaft||03-23-2001||06:55 PM||||I'm not sayin that all street racers are bad drivers. Hell, I street race too. But this kid drove his car fast everywhere and the accident was most likely not due to his driving ability. He was going a high rate of spped and lost control, most likely from hitting a pothole. (The raod is full of them) The topic was supposed to grab people's attention. I'm not saying never do it and everyone that drives fast is bad. I'm saying that this is a reson people should think before they put their foot down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||03-23-2001||07:13 PM||||I agree with you completely.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||03-23-2001||10:38 PM||||Insensitive... but thinning the herd.  There are so many out there that street race (especially in Flint) and or drive much too fast.<P>When one considers the ratio of rich idiots with fast cars compared to the sensible drivers at GMI is far greater than the national average.  Hopefully, the parking lots around campus (like it is really a campus) will empty out a bit slower in light of this event...<P>On a positive note, there sure as hell are a lot of GMI guys that have Fieros.  Jerediah Welti (on the list) was in my class... for that matter, there seem to be quite a few engineers that drive them.  That has to say something about them.  I do not know if it is good or bad, but certainly interesting.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||13
Z||000006||fierospeeder||03-24-2001||12:28 AM||||Can someone explain to me on how a person "loses control of a vehicle while driving"?<P>I understand if its Professional racing, like drag racing when the guy is losing traction from the beginning, or in nascar, the turns have tire debry and slides into the wall.<P>Besides mechanical failures, how did he wreck his car?  Going in and out of traffic and there was a banana peel and he lost traction.  <P>My girlfriend saw a chick in a camero last winter speeding, and she rolled her car over on a turn, the cause:  she was speeding with bald tires and her car didn't have enough traction plus she was drunk.  She pulled over with her sister to help her out, the driver said dont call the cops, while more people came, and one of them was an undercover cop.<P>A car is going to go straight unless you screw up, if he had rear wheel drive and it was wet, some of them will spin only one tire causing it to turn on you.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaft||03-24-2001||12:29 AM||||The only thing I don't like with your statement is that GMI students are a bunch of rich idiots. Yes, some are in rich families and their parents buy them nice cars. But a lot of us are here on scholarship, take out loans and use the money we make from our co-op job on a car. Considering most of us work for GM, Ford or one of their suppliers we get good discounts on new vehicles. And with no other expenses right now except school, a lot of us blow our money on a new car. There are just as many students here driving beaters as ther are driving nice cars. If we were all a bunch of rich kids that had nice cars why would some drive Fiero's. Don't get me wrong, I love these cars, but they are cheap on the used market and rich kids would rather have a new Camaro. Sorry if I sound like I'm rambeling, but don't take cheap shots by trying to insult my school or the people that go to it. You also say that a GMI kid that had a fiero was in your class. Did you go here? If you did, why do you seem so bitter at GMI students?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaft||03-24-2001||12:33 AM||||fierospeedster, I'm wondering the same thing. But if you drive around Flint you'd think you were driving on a cobblestone road.  One of my friends hit a pothole earler in Jan at about 20 mph and his tire went flat in about a minute. My guess is that he hit a pothole which caoused him to lose control.  Just my two cents.<p>[This message has been edited by Shaft (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||filthyscarecrow||03-25-2001||09:44 PM||||i'm sorry, but i have no sympathy for someone who isn't intelligent enough to wear a seatbelt and just happens to get killed. it takes 2 seconds to put on, and it's not uncomfortable at all. as far as i'm concerned, this is proof of evolution- survival of the fittest. he was too stoopid to wear a seatbelt, thus nature failed to select him. people i knew in high school got killed in accidents because they weren't wearing a seatbelt. while it saddened me that they were gone, i lost a lot of respect for them because of it. <BR>i don't think anyone deserves to die in a car accident, but i also don't think anyone has a right to expect anyone else to feel sorry for them if they were too stupid or lazy to take 2 seconds out of their day to prevent it. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||03-26-2001||02:11 AM||||Dude.. sometimes vehicles just lose control, be it driver error, or error in environment. Maybe you switched lanes a tiny bit to sharply, and there was gravel.. who knows.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||03-26-2001||10:01 PM||||Its a shame that one of your friends/fellow students had to go like that, but just because he drove fast everywhere all the time does not mean he had any more driving ability than anyone else.  I knew a guy who got a V-6 camaro when he turned 16 and drove like a freaking maniac.  He certianly did drive fast alot, but I sure wouldnt accuse him of having any exceptional level of skill.  He didnt die in an accident, but he did total one car and ruin two engines in another.  <P>Besides that, an 18 year old has no business driving around an automobile like a WS-6 Trans Am (at least without adult supervision).  Too much power + Too little experience = bad things.  <P>I know that the younger drivers on this board wont like that comment, but its a fact.  Some cars are just too much.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<P>"Who wants somma Debo?"||||reg||1
Z||000012||Galen||03-26-2001||11:52 PM||||Seatbelts are notuncomfortable? thats a bunch of bull. I'm only 6'3 and ALOT of seatbelts are uncomfortable. Steabelt should be optional. As for you loosing respect for someone who doesn't wear it, thats your choice. I dont wear mine alot (shhh My7Fieros) ... you gonna loose respect for me? I never asked for it in the first place. <P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - Sunroof, shaved emblems, more to come..<BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Black88GT||03-26-2001||11:58 PM||||I too used to think that seatbelts should be optional, until I looked at the political/financial aspect of it.  I forget the statistics, but you have a much better chance of getting seriously injured or killed without one.  Well what does that mean to you, you might ask.  It means a lot more tax money is going to be spent on losers who refuse to wear their seatbelts for whatever reason.  More tax money being spent equals more taxes for the masses!  Same rule applies with the helmet laws for motorcyclists...<P>As a personal experience a seatbelt has saved me from serious injury on 2 occasions.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||filthyscarecrow||03-27-2001||01:42 AM||||i'm sorry. <BR>i was wrong. i shouldn't call anyone who doesn't wear a seatbelt stupid.<BR>the only thing they should be called is organ donors.<P>i'm 6'1 and i have no problem with them. even if it is unconfortable, you can modify them so that they're not and still be just as effective. i don't think that seatbelts should be required. i think they should be optional. the only reason the human race hasn't evloved at all the last few centuries is because there are too many laws and social taboos against the means of evolution. i figure if they're not intelligent enough to know that they'll die in an accident without one, no big loss to the gene pool. seriously though, how friggin stupid do you have to be to NOT take 2 seconds out of your day to reduce your chance of injury or even prevent your own death? ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||03-28-2001||10:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Dude.. sometimes vehicles just lose control, be it driver error, or error in environment. Maybe you switched lanes a tiny bit to sharply, and there was gravel.. who knows.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I <I>hate</I> that phrase.<BR>A vehicle absolutely, positively, 100% CAN <I><B>NOT</I></B> "just lose control". There is <I>always</I> a cause. All too frequently-- as was the case in 99% of the Firestone/Explorer deaths--the cause is driver error, but there is <I>always</I> a cause.<P>When the Firestone debacle was still the headline of the day, I saw a woman interviewed from her hospital bed. She was a passenger. She said "we were driving along and just lost control of the car". I wanted to reach into the TV and slap her, quadruplegic or not.<P>I am so sure the cause of her accident was driver error I am writing it on the internet. The driver was not mentally prepared for such an emergency, not properly instructed in the correct course of action to pursue in the event of such an emergency, and panicked when the emergency came around.<P>BRING ON THE FLAMES!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000016||filthyscarecrow||03-29-2001||04:01 PM||||what flames? i'm with ya 100% on that one will...||||reg||1
Z||000017||william_thorniii||03-29-2001||10:06 PM||||Shaft,<P>I am not bitter, just not surprised or upset.  I was recently a student at GMI.  During my tour there, I was constantly amazed that more did not die in their often seeded adventures in and around Flint.  I stand by my previous statements that the majority of the student body has more $ than sense.  Par example, there was a gentleman in my class that purchased a new BMW 330 ci with CASH.  The problem is that most folks have tuition paid for by their families, and earn 15+K per year.  Take a look at the parking lots and prove me wrong.<P>My comment about it being interesting that so many GMI students are driving a Fiero is to point out how well they are/were engineered which is contrary to popular belief. <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR> - A.K.A. Resident *******  ||||reg||13
